<title>BELGIUM: SGB says debt falls to 20 bln Bfr.</title>
<headline>SGB says debt falls to 20 bln Bfr.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Belgian holding company Societe Generale de Belgique SA (SGB) said in a statement on Wednesday that its outstanding debt was 20.4 billion Belgian francs at the end of February 1997 compared with 30.4 billion at the end of 1996.</p>
<p>At the end of 1995 SGB had cash totalling 14.9 billion.</p>
<p>SGB said it spent in 1996 57.9 billion francs investing mainly in its Tractebel, Generale Bank, and Fortis AG units, while it raised 12.6 billion francs mainly from the sale of its stakes in Elf-Aquitaine and French Compagnie de Suez.</p>
<p>SGB, which is 63 percent owned by Suez, said so far in 1997 it had invested a further 3.1 billion francs in Fortis AG while raising 13.1 billion from the sale of its stake in Accor.</p>
